# {{PROJECT_NAME}} frontend agent guide

This is the React + Vite frontend for the counter contract in the parent
directory. Read the root `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `package.json`, and npm
lockfile when present. Run all commands below from the project root.

## Read the right guidance

Start with the project's installed dependencies and existing code. If they
leave questions, the Thruput skill's TypeScript and React references are optional
resources; discover them with `thruput skill --help`. Check examples against the
installed versions. Keep the provided client and wallet helpers unless the task
includes changing that architecture.

## Frontend layout

- `main.tsx`: counter UI, reads, and transaction feedback.
- `wallet.ts`: temporary native browser wallet and local funding.
- `refresh-counter.ts`: bounded read refresh after a confirmed transaction.
- `generated/counter.ts`: typed contract bindings, regenerated by build/deployment.
- `../scripts/thruput.mjs`: CLI runner and binding generation.
- `../scripts/deploy.mjs`: local wallet funding, deployment, and saved configuration.
- `../scripts/deployment.mjs`: load and verify the saved deployment.
- `../.thruput/app.json`: public configuration for the current local deployment.

Regenerate bindings after changing the contract interface. Do not edit generated
bindings or invent selectors, calldata encodings, or deployment addresses.

## Verify changes

- `npm run contract:build`: build the contract and generate its TypeScript bindings.
- `npm run typecheck`: check TypeScript. Generate bindings first with
  `npm run contract:build` if `web/generated/counter.ts` is missing or stale.
- `npm run build`: rebuild the contract and bindings, check TypeScript, and
  bundle the frontend. This needs Node/npm, the CLI, Rust, and npm registry access.
- For contract changes, also run the native and RV32 tests from the root guide.

To test the browser flow, follow `README.md`: start `thruput node`, install
the npm dependencies, run `npm run contract:deploy`, then `npm run dev`.
Deployment funds the configured local wallet and deploys a new counter. It is
not a read-only check. `npm run dev` uses the saved deployment without building
or deploying. Restarting Vite preserves the counter; deploy again after contract
changes or a node reset. A working build does not verify a live transaction.

## Local account and network behavior

The browser creates a temporary private key in memory and requests local test
funds. A reload creates a new wallet. Preserve this local example's signing
flow; do not substitute a wallet extension or switch to hosted Futurenet to follow
a general tutorial. Keep private signing material out of logs and files.

Read the deployment configuration supplied by the dev server. If a deployment
changes the RPC endpoint, restart `npm run dev`. The server supplies
`/deployment.json` and proxies `/rpc`; a static bundle needs those services to
work elsewhere. Refresh contract data after confirmed success, and retain the
original transaction hash when its result is uncertain.

After successful execution, wait for a view at or above the receipt height
before replacing the displayed counter value. Only stale reads are retried;
never repeat a transaction to refresh the UI or poll indefinitely.
